5f5bb9d1-db59-54f4-aa53-0a0fa0a6f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71935" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $N$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(N)=3\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(N)=0,$ we conclude that $N$ is not invertible. Since $N$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $N$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $V$ is a $5\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $Z$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Z)=0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 8=-16.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Z)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $Z$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is singular." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71935/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71935"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-316829/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "2c784e1e-459d-509b-9622-86a5898d3c23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-316829/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "588d26c3-4d17-54ae-b871-0fb44f1739ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-316829/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-316829/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-316829/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "9d7f55c7-be1d-5e79-b8af-61d91cb82c12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-316829/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b465e1f-321f-5e89-a053-c0bcd5500b59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-316829/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-316829/field-2/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-316829" :question/id #uuid "5de2acf0-9418-52f4-b9a6-27b23fa60b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-316829" :question/problem "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -x+7 \\\\ x-5 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot 0-(-x+7)\\cdot (x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x-7x+35 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =7,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-316829/field-1" "q-316829/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-316829"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
